// RED PIN (found by AoC day 12): a `<` inside an if condition makes the
// parser drop the ENTIRE argument (empty args in the AST → emitter writes
// `if ()`). Empirically isolated: `ch >= 48` parses, `ch <= 57` does not;
// `>` and `>=` survive. MECHANISM IS A GUESS (Claude, unverified): possibly
// the phantom-angle scan (`Type<state>`) hunting an unclosed `>`.
// ⚠ SYNTAX CAVEAT (Lars 2026-06-13): this pin was authored by Claude mid-
// grind — if it stays red after the `<` fix, REVIEW TOGETHER whether the
// pin's own spelling is at fault before trusting its claim.
// Workaround in the corpus: flip operands (57 >= ch). Expected: "lt".

~import std/io

const x: i64 = 3;

~if(x < 5)
| then |> std/io:print.ln("lt")
| else |> std/io:print.ln("ge")
